      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "$$\n\nGiven that $z>0,$ which of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z)?$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\nThe definition of $\\operatorname{sech}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡z) & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{z}^{-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{z+(z)^{-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(z+\\frac{1}{z})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\frac{{z}^{2}+1}{z})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
